{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% import '_toolbox.html.twig' as toolbox_ui %}

{% block title %}{{ chat.title }} — TaskLoom{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<header class="page-header">
    <h1>{{ chat.title }}</h1>
    <p class="muted">started {{ chat.createdAt|date('Y-m-d H:i') }}</p>
</header>

{% for label, messages in app.flashes %}
    {% for message in messages %}
        <p class="flash flash-{{ label }}" role="status">{{ message }}</p>
    {% endfor %}
{% endfor %}

{#
    The transcript, rendered from the ledger. Each turn carries its speaker as
    a typed column (not a payload entry), so this loop is a plain read of the
    conversational events — which is the whole point of having no separate
    turn table (SPEC §15).

    The human is labelled "You" and the assistant "Nia" here, in the UI. That
    is the *reader's* view; the model's view is roles, with no visible name
    prefix (SPEC §15 §2.5) — the two are deliberately different renderings of
    the same stored turn.

    A tool call renders as a line of its own rather than as a bubble: it is
    something she *did*, not something she said, and the result is not shown
    at all — it is already reflected in the reply, and it is often long. The
    full row is in the ledger for anyone who needs it.
#}
<section aria-labelledby="transcript-heading" class="chat-transcript">
    <h2 id="transcript-heading" class="visually-hidden">Transcript</h2>

    {#
        The window's confession (SPEC §15.9).

        A conversation grows forever; the model's window does not. When the
        oldest turns have slipped out of the model's view, this says so in
        plain words — the ledger records every trim, but a person needs the
        consequence, not the row: *she can no longer see the start of this
        conversation*. It changes what it makes sense to ask for without
        restating context, which is exactly the kind of thing that is worse
        to leave unsaid.
    #}
    {% if window_notice is not null %}
        <p class="muted" role="status">{{ window_notice }}</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% if transcript is empty %}
        <p class="muted">Nothing said yet.</p>
    {% endif %}

    {% for turn in transcript %}
        {% if turn.type.value == 'tool_call' %}
            {#
                One line per ROUND, each tool named once.

                A row carries every call of one round, and the model may ask for
                the same tool twice in that round — so "used echo, echo" was
                rendering, which reads like a bug when it is a real second call.
                The distinct names say what the round did; the individual
                `tool_result` rows are in the ledger for anyone who needs the
                count.

                No link: the catalog page is the only tool surface the app has,
                and pointing every name at the same URL would look like it went
                somewhere tool-specific. If a per-tool page ever exists, this is
                where the name becomes a link to it.
            #}
            {% set names = [] %}
            {% for call in turn.payload.calls|default([]) %}
                {% set name = call.name|default('') %}
                {% if name != '' and name not in names %}{% set names = names|merge([name]) %}{% endif %}
            {% endfor %}
            <p class="chat-tool muted">
                used
                {% for name in names %}<code>{{ name }}</code>{% if not loop.last %}<span class="tool-sep">·</span>{% endif %}{% endfor %}
                <span class="muted">{{ turn.createdAt|date('H:i') }}</span>
            </p>

        {% else %}
            <article class="chat-turn chat-turn-{{ turn.speaker.value }}">
                <p class="chat-speaker">
                    {{ turn.speaker.value == 'nia' ? 'Nia' : 'You' }}
                    <span class="muted">{{ turn.createdAt|date('H:i') }}</span>
                </p>
                <div class="chat-bubble md">{{ turn.content|markdown }}</div>
            </article>
        {% endif %}
    {% endfor %}
</section>

{% if pending %}
    <p class="notice" role="status">Nia is thinking… <a href="{{ path('app_chat_show', {id: chat.id}) }}">refresh</a></p>
{% endif %}

{#
    A failed exchange says so, out loud.

    This is the one failure the run lanes have no precedent for: a run that
    dies is visible in the run history, and a chat turn that dies is a person
    watching an empty space where a reply should be. The classified reason is
    recorded in the ledger, so it can be shown rather than guessed at.
#}
{% if lastError is not null %}
    <p class="flash flash-error" role="alert">
        I couldn't get a turn: {{ lastError }}
    </p>
{% endif %}

{#
    Saying something, with the tools for the exchange it starts.

    The picker is the same macro the task editor uses (SPEC §4.1), with the
    same field names — so what a checked box means is decided in one place
    (`App\\Admin\\ToolboxSelection`), and the widget cannot drift from the parser.

    **Disabled while a reply is pending**, deliberately, rather than accepting
    a change it would ignore: the toolbox freezes when the exchange starts
    (`CHAT_TOOLS.md` §2), so the tools shown are the ones she is actually
    using right now. A control that silently does nothing would be worse.

    The picker always reopens showing your **last answer** — the running
    exchange's frozen set while a reply is pending, otherwise the previous
    exchange's choice, pre-filling the next message. So what you see ticked is
    what you chose, and what she can do is never a thing you have to remember.

    COLLAPSED, the same as the new-conversation picker (`_toolbox.html.twig`
    explains the `collapsed` argument): the form is a message box with a
    permission panel folded above the Send button, not the other way round.
    The disclosure is legitimate alongside the `disabled` flag above — a shut
    drawer still carries the frozen toolbox in its summary and its hidden
    inputs, so nothing is lost by folding it, and a hand-rolled POST that tries
    to change the toolbox mid-reply is refused by the controller either way.

    That includes *unticking everything*: if the last exchange ran with no
    tools, this renders nothing ticked. That is a real answer being carried,
    not an absence of one — which is why the engine freezes an empty
    declaration rather than leaving the column NULL.
#}
<form method="post" action="{{ path('app_chat_say', {id: chat.id}) }}" class="chat-compose">
    <input type="hidden" name="_token" value="{{ csrf_token('chat-say-' ~ chat.id) }}">

    {{ toolbox_ui.toolbox(
        '',
        toolbox_values|default({toolbox_mode: 'explicit', toolbox: []}),
        known_tags,
        catalog_tools,
        errors|default({}),
        'chat_toolbox',
        pending,
        true
    ) }}

    <label for="say-message" class="visually-hidden">Message</label>
    <textarea id="say-message" name="message" rows="3" required
              placeholder="Say something…" autocomplete="off" {{ pending ? 'disabled' }}></textarea>
    <button type="submit" {{ pending ? 'disabled' }}>Send</button>
</form>

<p class="page-back"><a href="{{ path('app_chat_list') }}">← All conversations</a></p>
{% endblock %}
